<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mantinea</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 223 BCE (approx.) · capital Mantinea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Democratic Arcadian city that twice gave its name to great battles and was twice destroyed for opposing Sparta and Macedon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mantinea grew from the union of several villages on the high plain of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, a synoecism usually dated to around 500 BCE or soon after. Its constant rival was <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tegea">Tegea</a>, which shared the same plain and its scarce water. Mantinea was normally a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> but resented Spartan control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unusually for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, Mantinea had a moderate democracy, admired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, in which farmers voted but elected officials did the governing. It was famous for its well-drilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>. In 418 BCE it joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> and was defeated at the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-418-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a>; in 385 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> broke the city into villages by damming the river Ophis against its walls.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Refounded in 370 BCE, Mantinea helped create the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arcadian-league">Arcadian League</a>, and in 362 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> died winning the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-418-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a>. After it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III of Sparta</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus Doson</a> destroyed it in 223 BCE and renamed it Antigoneia. Hadrian later restored the old name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mantinea.jpg" alt="Mantinea" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DE_Mandinias.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 223 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mantinea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Arcadian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mantineia, Antigoneia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-362&amp;focus=mantinea" aria-label="Mantinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5239 824 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mantinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5559 1080L5562 1098M5559 1080L5553 1087M5559 1080L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5553 1087h0M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5559 1080h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5559 1080h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-362&amp;focus=mantinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

